            "A two-year-old brought to a health centre with 'fever' may have malaria, pneumonia, early dehydration from the diarrhoea the mother did not mention, moderate malnutrition and a missed measles vaccine — and treating the malaria alone sends her home with four problems. <strong>Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI)</strong>, used in Ghana since the late 1990s at every first-level facility, is a structured way of assessing a sick child from two months to five years (a separate chart covers the young infant under two months) that <strong>looks for all the common killers in every child</strong>, classifies each problem by severity on a traffic-light scale, and ties each classification to a treatment, a counselling message and a follow-up date. It works because it assumes the nurse has no laboratory, little time and a mother who will not come back unless told why — which is exactly the situation at most CHPS compounds — and it is the framework behind most paediatric questions in the licensing examination.",
