            "Malaria causes roughly a third of outpatient attendances and remains among the largest killers of Ghanaian children under five, concentrated in the under-fives and in the rainy seasons; <em>Plasmodium falciparum</em> accounts for nearly all of it. Because fever has many causes and malaria treatment should not be given on guesswork, Ghana's policy is <strong>test every fever</strong> — a rapid diagnostic test (RDT) or microscopy — and <strong>treat only confirmed cases</strong>. A negative test in a child with fever means look for the other cause (pneumonia, ear, urine, measles, typhoid, meningitis) and review in two days; a positive test in a child without danger signs is <strong>uncomplicated malaria</strong>, treated at home with an <strong>artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT)</strong> — artemether–lumefantrine (AL) twice daily for three days given with food or milk, or artesunate–amodiaquine once daily for three days, or dihydroartemisinin–piperaquine — dosed <strong>by weight band</strong> (AL: 5–<15 kg one tablet, 15–<25 kg two, 25–<35 kg three, 35 kg and over four tablets per dose, six doses over three days), with paracetamol for the fever, fluids and feeding continued, the mother shown how to give the first dose and told to <strong>return if the child cannot drink, vomits everything, becomes drowsy, convulses or is not better in three days</strong>. Chloroquine, sulfadoxine–pyrimethamine monotherapy and artemisinin monotherapy are not used for treatment; SP is reserved for intermittent preventive treatment in pregnancy and, in the northern regions, for seasonal malaria chemoprevention in children 3–59 months.",
