          paragraphs: [
            "Diarrhoea — three or more loose or watery stools in 24 hours — is almost always infectious in Ghanaian children: rotavirus (now in the EPI), <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Shigella</em>, <em>Campylobacter</em>, <em>Salmonella</em>, cholera in outbreaks, and <em>Giardia</em> and <em>Cryptosporidium</em> in persistent diarrhoea and HIV. It kills by <strong>dehydration</strong> and by the malnutrition that follows repeated episodes, and the two interventions that have halved its mortality cost almost nothing: <strong>low-osmolarity oral rehydration salts (ORS)</strong>, which uses the glucose-coupled sodium transport that diarrhoea leaves intact to pull water back into the body, and <strong>zinc</strong>, which shortens the episode and prevents the next one. Antidiarrhoeals, antimotility drugs (loperamide), kaolin, 'stop' medicines and most antibiotics have no place in a child's diarrhoea and some are dangerous.",
            "Assessment follows the IMCI sequence (Lesson 4.1): how long, blood in the stool, and the four signs of dehydration — the child's <strong>general condition</strong> (well and alert; restless or irritable; lethargic or unconscious), <strong>sunken eyes</strong> (ask the mother whether the eyes look different), <strong>thirst</strong> when offered fluid (drinks normally; drinks eagerly; not able to drink or drinks poorly) and the <strong>skin pinch</strong> over the abdomen (goes back immediately; slowly; very slowly over two seconds). Two signs from the severe column make severe dehydration; two from the middle column make some dehydration; fewer means no dehydration. Weight is the best measure of fluid lost — weigh every child with diarrhoea and again after rehydration.",
          ],
