          alt: "Severe acute malnutrition — diagnosis and the first week. Diagnosis in a child six to fifty-nine months: MUAC under 11.5 cm, or weight for height below minus 3 z-scores, or bilateral pitting oedema. Two forms: marasmus — severe wasting, old-man face, loose skin folds, visible ribs, alert and hungry; kwashiorkor — oedema, moon face, flaky paint dermatosis, sparse pale hair, enlarged liver, apathetic and miserable. Triage: an appetite test with RUTF — passes and has no complications, outpatient CMAM with RUTF weekly; fails or has complications (oedema plus plus plus, no appetite, danger signs, pneumonia, dehydration, hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, severe anaemia), inpatient stabilisation. The ten steps: treat and prevent hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and dehydration, correct electrolytes, treat infection with routine antibiotics, correct micronutrients without iron at first, begin cautious feeding with F-75, then catch-up growth with F-100 or RUTF, sensory stimulation, and prepare for follow-up. Dangers: standard ORS and intravenous fluids (heart failure — use ReSoMal slowly), too much food too fast (refeeding), iron in the first week, and missing the hypothermic, hypoglycaemic, silent septic child who shows no fever.",
