            "<strong>Community-based Management of Acute Malnutrition (CMAM)</strong> — Ghana's national approach since 2008 — treats the majority of children with severe acute malnutrition <strong>at home</strong>: the child with SAM who <strong>passes the appetite test</strong> (eats a test portion of RUTF in the clinic within 30 minutes), has no medical complications and no severe oedema is enrolled at the outpatient care site, given routine amoxicillin, a measles check, vitamin A if due, deworming after the first visit, and a weekly ration of <strong>ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF)</strong> — a peanut-based paste in 92 g sachets of about 500 kcal, needing no water or cooking, at roughly 200 kcal/kg/day (an 8 kg child needs about three sachets a day) — with weekly weighing and MUAC until discharge. The mother is taught that <strong>RUTF is a medicine for this child</strong>, not food for the family; that clean water is offered with it; that breastfeeding continues first; that the child returns at once if she stops eating, becomes febrile, develops oedema or has diarrhoea; and the community volunteers who screen with MUAC tapes find the children who do not come. The child who fails the appetite test, has complications or is under six months goes to inpatient stabilisation and returns to CMAM when she is eating.",
            "<strong>Moderate acute malnutrition</strong> (MUAC 11.5–12.4 cm or weight for height −3 to −2) is treated with feeding counselling, a supplementary ration where a programme provides it (fortified blended flour or ready-to-use supplementary food), treatment of infection, deworming, vitamin A and fortnightly weighing — and is the stage at which a nurse's feeding counselling prevents a SAM admission. <strong>Anaemia</strong> (palmar pallor, haemoglobin under 11 g/dL in 6–59 months) is iron and folate for three months with deworming and malaria treatment, with severe pallor or haemoglobin under 5 referred for transfusion; sickle cell disease is tested for in every anaemic child whose anaemia does not respond.",
