            "Most malnutrition in Ghana begins between <strong>six and eighteen months</strong>, when breast milk alone is no longer enough and the complementary foods offered are too thin, too few and too late. The counselling is specific and uses the family's own foods. <strong>From six months</strong>: start with two to three tablespoons of <strong>thick</strong> porridge or mashed food twice or three times a day and increase to half a cup by eight months; enrich koko, tuo zaafi, banku or mashed yam with <strong>groundnut paste, soya or bean flour, egg, powdered dried fish, mashed kontomire or other dark green leaves, orange-fleshed sweet potato, pawpaw, mango and a spoon of oil or palm oil</strong>; add a little of the family's soup without pepper. <strong>Nine to eleven months</strong>: finely chopped family foods and finger foods, three to four meals and a snack, half a cup each. <strong>From twelve months</strong>: the family diet, three to four meals and two snacks, three-quarters to a full cup, with <strong>an animal-source food — egg, fish, meat, milk — every day</strong> where possible, and breastfeeding continued to two years. Use the <strong>child's own bowl</strong> so the mother sees what was eaten; feed <strong>responsively</strong> — slowly, patiently, encouraging and talking, never forcing or leaving a toddler alone with a plate; wash hands and utensils; and during and after illness offer more frequent, softer, favourite foods and an extra meal a day for two weeks.",
